    "narrative": "The primary engine for Ghost Ride the WHIP today is **Corbin Carroll**, who leads the squad with a 73.39 efficiency score against the favorable matchup of Holmes. While the opposing battery offers an elite hold\u2014which suppresses his stolen base upside by 4%\u2014his raw speed and premium slot in the two-hole make him an essential anchor. Alongside him, **Vinnie Pasquantino** (72.92) is our strongest recommendation; he faces a soft 5.31 xERA arm in Scherzer. The 19% boost from the pitcher\u2019s lack of skill, combined with a dynamic platoon advantage, positions **Vinnie Pasquantino** for a high-ceiling performance that could effectively carry our RBI and R categories single-handedly.\n\nStrategic benching remains critical, with **Austin Riley** (41.01) standing as the most difficult omission. While **Austin Riley** was considered, he falls just short of the 60+ threshold due to a daunting matchup against Glasnow, where a 19% penalty for the opposing pitcher\u2019s skill and a drop to the eight-hole cratered his projection. Conversely, we are keeping **Jose Caballero** (58.58) active despite his poor batting position because our league\u2019s obsession with stolen bases forces us to prioritize his sprint profile over safer, lower-upside bench options like **Cole Young** (38.58) or **Griffin Conine** (32.10), both of whom were sent to the bench for failing to clear the sub-40 floor. \n\nWe must closely monitor the weather and final lineups, as the marginal score for **Zach McKinstry** (47.85) and **Mickey Moniak** (49.10) leaves them vulnerable. **Mickey Moniak** is particularly at risk today; despite his utility, he faces a 15% platoon penalty against Alvarez. If the wind shifts or late lineup adjustments push these hitters further down the order, we may need to reconsider our leverage. Until then, we are banking on the high-floor production of **Seiya Suzuki** (65.61) and **Isaac Paredes** (60.65) to insulate our core stats against the volatility of the day\u2019s more speculative starts.",
